module examples.err_cap_provenance_enum

# plans/M7.md item I (the adversarial sweep), 03-hardware.md §1: "The
# compiler checks provenance transitively — a function that touches MMIO,
# DMA, or IRQ state must be reachable through the owning driver's
# authority."
#
# `golden/err-cap-provenance` is this program with the capability spelled
# bare (`vector: IrqCap[u32]`). Here it arrives inside an **enum variant
# payload**, and the check went quiet.
#
# The mechanism is worth stating exactly, because the dead code was
# already written and looked right:
# `sema::types::capability_authority` has an explicit `DeclItem::Enum`
# arm whose job is to put a capability-bearing enum's own name into
# `capability_bearing` — the flattened set `eval::legal::capability_in_type`
# reads. That arm could never fire: it asked `contains_capability`, which
# consulted a struct-only map, so `structs.get(<enum name>)` was always
# `None`. No enum ever entered the set, so no fn holding one ever counted
# as touching hardware state, so provenance had nothing to authorize.
# One shared table (`components_by_name`) is what makes the arm real.
#
# The accepting sibling is `golden/check-capabilities`: there the driver's
# own method calls the helper, so the helper is reachable through the
# driver's authority.

enum Held:
    Bound(IrqCap[u32])
    Unbound(u32)

@driver
pub struct BlkDriver:
    sectors: u64

    fn service(read self) -> u64:
        return self.sectors

fn ack_vector(read held: Held) -> u64:
    return 1
